    "title": "Trump pauses Jones Act, what it means for gas prices",
    "text": "March 19, 2026, 12:25 p.m. ETPresident Donald Trump temporarily halted the Jones Act to help ease the cost of oil, gas an other commodities amid the armed conflict with Iran in the Strait of Hormuz that has push oil prices up.Here's what to know.What's the Jones Act?The Jones Act, or the Merchant Marine Act, is a law from 1920 that regulates shipping between U.S. seaports.The law requires that only American ships carry cargo between American ports. That means that any cargo ship transporting merchandise between U.S. seaports, must be a vessel built in the U.S., owned by a U.S. company, crewed mainly by U.S. citizens, and flagged in the U.S.The law doesn't affect continental United States much, as most transportation of goods happens via ground. The Jones Act mainly affects cargo to and from Puerto Rico, Hawaii, Alaska and Guam.This 1920 law was created after World War I to reduce dependency on foreign ships in times of war and strengthen the shipping industry.Cargo carried between a port in a foreign country and the U.S. does not need to meet Jones Act requirements. So a ship carrying goods from the U.K. into the U.S., for example, doesn't need to be an American ship.Did Trump suspend the Jones Act?White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt said on X that the 60-day waiver is meant to “mitigate the short-term disruptions to the oil market as the U.S. military continues meeting the objectives of Operation Epic Fury.\"U.S. freight fleet is more expensive than foreign cargo ship companies.The Jones Act waiver could potentially reduce East Coast gasoline prices by three cents per gallon, but could raise costs on the Gulf Coast, the Center for American Progress stated in an assesment of the measure.Juan Carlos Castillo is a New Jersey-based trending reporter for the USA Today Network.",
